---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const inverseCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '2^x', label: 'y = 2ˣ', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: 'log(x)/log(2)', label: 'y = log₂x', color: '#b42318' },
    { expr: 'x', label: 'the mirror line y = x', color: 'rgba(23,36,32,0.4)', width: 1.2 },
  ],
  x: { min: -4, max: 6, label: 'x' },
  y: { min: -4, max: 6 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};

const expLogMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each statement with what it says:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "log₂32 = 5",
      "right": "the power I must raise 2 to, to get 32"
    },
    {
      "left": "log₁₀1,000 = 3",
      "right": "10³ = 1,000"
    },
    {
      "left": "bʸ = x",
      "right": "log_b(x) = y"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The log is the exponent — the exponential's undo button, and its mirror image across the line y = x."
};

const expExtSort = {
  "prompt": "File each extension under the requirement that forces it:",
  "buckets": [
    "a⁰ = 1",
    "a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ",
    "a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "because aⁿ ÷ aⁿ must equal aⁿ⁻ⁿ",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "because aⁿ × a⁻ⁿ must equal a⁰ = 1",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "because (a^(1/2))² must equal a",
      "bucket": 2
    }
  ],
  "why": "The extensions were chosen to preserve the index laws — not invented as tricks."
};

const expZeroSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these claims about zero exponents is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "2⁴ = 16, 2³ = 8, 2² = 4, 2¹ = 2.",
    "Divide by 2 each step.",
    "The next value must be 1.",
    "a⁰ = 0, because multiplying zero times gives nothing."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "a⁰ = 1 — any other definition would break every index law at once.",
  "why": "The pattern makes the answer obvious: divide by 2 each step and the next value must be 1."
};

const expHeartEst = {
  "prompt": "Estimate: 70 beats per minute × 60 × 24 × 365 × 80 years — roughly how many heartbeats in a lifetime?",
  "unit": "",
  "answer": 3000000000,
  "factor": 1.5,
  "why": "About 3 × 10⁹ — estimation problems are standard-form arithmetic plus nerve, exactly how professional scientists sanity-check claims.",
  "context": "orders of magnitude: a rat is 10² times a mouse, a human 10² times a rat"
};

const expLawCompare = {
  "prompt": "The same law, two directions — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "growth",
    "text": "A = A₀(1 + r)ⁿ — constant percentage means ever-larger absolute change, concave-up"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "decay",
    "text": "N = N₀(½)^(t/T) — halves whatever remains, never quite reaching zero"
  },
  "why": "Decay keeps a schedule for halves, not for the last atom — the horizontal asymptote in action."
};

const expCarbonEst = {
  "prompt": "Charcoal holds 25% of the living carbon-14 ratio (half-life 5,730 years). Roughly how old is the sample?",
  "unit": "years",
  "answer": 11460,
  "factor": 1.25,
  "why": "25% is two halvings, so 2 × 5,730 = 11,460 years — archaeology's clock is exponential decay plus a logarithm.",
  "context": "at 12.5%, three halvings: 17,190 years"
};

const expLawCloze = {
  "text": "The log laws: log(ab) = log a %%0%% log b — multiplying becomes adding; log(a/b) = log a − log b — division becomes subtraction; and log(aⁿ) = %%1%%·log a — powers become coefficients.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "+",
      "plus"
    ],
    [
      "n",
      "the exponent"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "What sign the product law uses, and what factor the power law produces."
};

const expSumSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these log laws is fake — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "log(ab) = log a + log b — multiplying becomes adding.",
    "log(a/b) = log a − log b.",
    "log(aⁿ) = n·log a.",
    "log(a + b) = log a + log b."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "There is no law for log(a + b) — combine the addition first, then log.",
  "why": "Check with a = b = 1: log 2 ≈ 0.301 but log 1 + log 1 = 0."
};

const expSolveBranch = {
  "root": "Solve 3ˣ = 20. A student writes x = log 20 − log 3 ≈ 0.82. What went wrong?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Diagnose the move:",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Nothing — the subtraction is the right move",
          "next": "wrong",
          "feedback": "The power law says log(aⁿ) = n·log a — but here the unknown is the exponent: x = log 20 / log 3 ≈ 2.73, division not subtraction."
        },
        {
          "label": "The exponent comes out as a ratio of logs: x = log 20 / log 3 ≈ 2.73",
          "next": "right",
          "feedback": "Right — take log of both sides, apply the power law, and divide."
        },
        {
          "label": "Try integer values first",
          "next": "guess",
          "feedback": "Guessing works for 3ˣ = 27 but not 20 — the log route is general."
        }
      ]
    },
    "wrong": {
      "text": "Subtracting logs belongs to log(a/b), not to an unknown exponent."
    },
    "right": {
      "text": "n = log(goal/start) / log(1 + r) — one formula for every 'how long?' question growth can pose."
    },
    "guess": {
      "text": "The log route answers every case, integer or not."
    }
  },
  "why": "Solving exponential equations is the payoff: log both sides, apply the power law, divide."
};

const expQuakePlace = {
  "prompt": "On the earthquake magnitude scale, each whole number is roughly how many times more energy released?",
  "labels": [
    "×2",
    "×10",
    "×32",
    "×100"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 1,
    "max": 100
  },
  "answer": 32,
  "tolerance": 3,
  "why": "About 32-fold per whole number — a magnitude 7 is not 'a bit worse' than a 6, it is a different category of event."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/intermediate/exponentials-and-logarithms">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Mathematics lesson. It assumes the foundational Linear Functions
    lesson (you should be at home reading straight-line graphs), Quadratics, and Ratios,
    Percentages and Proportion — constant percentage change is the seed of everything here.
    Equations and Inequalities supplies the rearranging practice. Powers should already feel
    familiar as repeated multiplication; this chapter completes them rather than restarting.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>apply the index laws, including zero, negative and fractional exponents, and say why each extension keeps the laws true;</li>
    <li>move fluently between standard form, orders of magnitude and order-of-magnitude estimation;</li>
    <li>model constant-percentage growth and decay, including doubling time and half-life;</li>
    <li>use the three log laws to compress products into sums and to solve exponential equations;</li>
    <li>interpret log scales — pH, earthquake magnitude, decibels — without being fooled by them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Exponentials answer "what happens with constant <em>ratio</em> growth?" — each step
    multiplies, rather than adds. Logarithms are their undo button: <strong>the log is the
    exponent</strong>. If 2⁵ = 32, then log₂32 = 5 — "the power I must raise 2 to, to get 32".
    The two are inverse functions, mirror images across the line y = x (see the widget below),
    and together they run the quantitative parts of science: population growth, radioactive
    decay, earthquake sizes, acidity, decibels, algorithm runtimes. One pair of ideas, an
    enormous range of jobs.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="pair each statement with its translation" widget="match" config={expLogMatch} />
  <p>
    This chapter also completes the index laws. Powers were introduced as repeated multiplication;
    the full rules extend them to fractional and negative exponents, and to the number e —
    the base the universe seems to prefer. Master the laws, and logarithms stop being a new
    topic and become the exponent laws, read backwards.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="each curve is the other reflected — growth up, growth down, one mirror between them" widget="plotter" config={inverseCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the purple exponential and the red logarithm are the same
    shape folded across the dashed mirror line y = x: whatever height the growth curve reaches
    at a given x, the log curve reaches at the swapped coordinates. Trace a point on one curve
    across to its reflection on the other — that symmetry is what "inverse functions" means,
    and it is why the log undoes exactly what the exponential does.
  </p>

  <h2>Powers, extended: the index laws and their logic</h2>
  <p>
    The laws are few and non-negotiable: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ, (ab)ⁿ =
    aⁿbⁿ. Each is forced by what "repeated multiplication" means — you can verify aᵐ⁺ⁿ by
    counting the factors. The extensions are chosen to <em>preserve</em> the laws:
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="file each extension under the law that forces it" widget="sort-buckets" config={expExtSort} />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>a⁰ = 1</strong>, because aⁿ ÷ aⁿ must equal aⁿ⁻ⁿ = a⁰, and anything divided by
    itself is 1.</li>
    <li><strong>a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ</strong>, because aⁿ × a⁻ⁿ must equal a⁰ = 1.</li>
    <li><strong>a^(1/2) = √a and a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a</strong>, because (a^(1/2))² must equal a.</li>
    <li><strong>a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ</strong> — take the root, then the power (roots first keeps the
    arithmetic small).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Worked example: simplify 27^(2/3). The cube root of 27 is 3, and 3² = 9 — done. Or 16^(−3/4)
    = 1/(16^(3/4)) = 1/(2³) = ⅛. These are not tricks; they are the definitions doing their one
    job, keeping the laws true.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a⁰ = 0 "because multiplying zero times gives nothing". The
  pattern makes the answer obvious instead: 2⁴ = 16, 2³ = 8, 2² = 4, 2¹ = 2 — divide by 2 each
  step, and the next value <em>must</em> be 1. Any other definition of a⁰ would break every
  index law at once.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the pattern-breaker" widget="spot-error" config={expZeroSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the index laws are few, and every extension — zero, negative,
    fractional exponents — was chosen to keep them true. Next, what those exponents buy when
    the numbers get astronomically large or uncomfortably small.
  </p>

  <h2>Standard form and orders of magnitude</h2>
  <p>
    Science spans numbers from 0.00000000000000000000000166 kg (a hydrogen atom) to
    9.46 × 10¹⁵ m (a light-year). <strong>Standard form</strong> — a × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ a above 10 —
    makes them writable, comparable and computable: multiply the a-parts, add the exponents,
    renormalise. (2 × 10⁶) × (3 × 10⁻⁴) = 6 × 10². The deeper skill is <strong>orders of
    magnitude</strong> thinking: a rat is about 10² times heavier than a mouse, a human 10²
    times a rat, an elephant 10² times a human — life's mass ladder in steps of a hundred.
    Estimation problems (how many heartbeats in a lifetime? about 70 bpm × 60 × 24 × 365 × 80 ≈
    3 × 10⁹) are standard-form arithmetic plus nerve, and they are exactly how professional
    scientists sanity-check claims before computing anything precisely.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the lifetime heartbeat count" widget="estimate" config={expHeartEst} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> standard form makes the enormous writable, and orders of magnitude
    make it comparable. Now the rules run in time rather than in notation: what happens when
    the same percentage change compounds step after step.
  </p>

  <h2>Exponential growth and decay</h2>
  <p>
    Any process with constant <em>percentage</em> change per step is exponential:
    <strong>A = A₀(1 + r)ⁿ</strong>. Money at 5% for 10 years: 1000 × 1.05¹⁰ ≈ 1,629. A
    population falling 8% per year: × 0.92ⁿ. The counter-intuitive core is that constant
    <em>percentage</em> means ever-larger <em>absolute</em> change: 5% of 1,629 is far more than
    5% of 1,000. Graphs of exponentials have two signature features: they never touch zero on
    the decaying side (a horizontal asymptote — you can halve forever and never reach nothing),
    and they are concave-up on the growing side (the derivative page's eˣ section explains why).
  </p>
  <p>
    The special base <strong>e ≈ 2.71828</strong> arises when growth is compounded
    <em>continuously</em>: A = A₀e^(rt), with r the continuous rate. For anything with
    continuous rate r, the <strong>doubling time</strong> is ln 2 / r ≈ 0.693/r — the honest
    version of the economists' "rule of 70": divide 70 by the percentage growth rate to get the
    doubling time in years. A country growing 2% per year doubles in about 35 years; that one
    line is most of demography.
  </p>
  <p>
    Decay is the same law running backwards. Radioactive decay follows
    <strong>N = N₀·(½)^(t/T)</strong> with T the half-life — the time for half the atoms to
    decay. Worked example (carbon dating): living wood holds a fixed ratio of carbon-14
    (half-life 5,730 years); a charcoal sample from a fire pit holds 25% of that ratio. 25% is
    two halvings, so the sample is 2 × 5,730 = <strong>11,460 years old</strong>. At 12.5%,
    three halvings: 17,190 years. Archaeology's clock is exponential decay plus a logarithm.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="growth versus decay — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={expLawCompare} />
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the charcoal's age" widget="estimate" config={expCarbonEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> after two half-lives, all the atoms have decayed. Each half-life halves whatever remains — 100% becomes 50%, then 25%, then 12.5% — so the sample never quite reaches zero (the decaying curve's horizontal asymptote in action). "How long until it is all gone?" has no answer for a pure exponential: decay keeps a schedule for halves, not for the last atom.</p>

  <h2>Logarithms: the exponent, isolated</h2>
  <p>
    The definition does all the work: <strong>log_b(x) = y means bʸ = x</strong>. Logs take a
    product and return an exponent; that is why log₂8 = 3 and log₁₀1,000 = 3. The widget above
    shows the geometry: y = 2ˣ and y = log₂x are reflections in y = x — whatever one does, the
    other undoes. Three laws follow immediately from the index laws, and they are the subject's
    entire toolkit:
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="fill the blanks — the log laws" widget="cloze" config={expLawCloze} />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>log(ab) = log a + log b</strong> — multiplying becomes adding. This single fact
    powered slide rules (align two rulers, add lengths, read products) and is why log-scale
    plots turn exponentials into straight lines.</li>
    <li><strong>log(a/b) = log a − log b</strong> — division becomes subtraction.</li>
    <li><strong>log(aⁿ) = n·log a</strong> — powers become coefficients. This is the law that
    untangles exponents from equations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> logs distribute over addition. log(a + b) is <strong>not</strong>
    log a + log b — check with a = b = 1: log 2 ≈ 0.301 (base 10) but log 1 + log 1 = 0. There
    is no law for log(a + b); when it appears in a problem, the intended move is almost always
    "combine the addition first, then log".
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the law that does not exist" widget="spot-error" config={expSumSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>Solving exponential equations</strong> is the payoff. Solve 3ˣ = 20: take log of
    both sides, apply the power law, and x = log 20 / log 3 ≈ 1.301/0.477 ≈ <strong>2.73</strong>.
    Solve "when does the money double?" for 1000 × 1.05ⁿ: 1.05ⁿ = 2, so n = log 2 / log 1.05 ≈
    0.301/0.0212 ≈ <strong>14.2 years</strong>. In general, n = log(goal/start) / log(1 + r) —
    one formula that answers every "how long?" question growth can pose.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="the exponent hunt" widget="branch" config={expSolveBranch} />

  <p>
    <strong>Changing base, and a medical worked example.</strong> Calculators carry two logs
    (base 10 and base e), so the <strong>change-of-base formula</strong> — log_b x = log x /
    log b — unlocks every other base: log₂1,000,000 = log 1,000,000 / log 2 ≈ 19.93, telling
    you a million is a hair under 2²⁰. (The handy anchor behind that: 2¹⁰ = 1024 ≈ 10³, so
    "every ten powers of two is about three powers of ten" — the reason RAM comes in 1s, 2s, 4s
    … and disk sizes in 1,000s, and why the two conventions famously disagree about what a
    "kilo"byte is.) Radioactive iodine-131, used to treat overactive thyroids, has a half-life
    of 8 days: after 24 days, 3 half-lives, 12.5% remains — and the hospital can compute the
    patient's remaining dose on any future date with exactly the N₀(½)^(t/T) formula above.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> logs isolate exponents, their three laws are the index laws read
    backwards, and change of base puts every base on a two-button calculator. What remains is
    the payoff in the wild: the scales science actually measures on.
  </p>

  <h2>Logarithmic scales: measuring the enormous</h2>
  <p>
    Human senses and natural phenomena both span orders of magnitude, so both get log scales.
    <strong>pH</strong> is −log₁₀ of hydrogen ion concentration: lemon juice at pH 2 has 10
    times the ion concentration of pH 3 wine, and 10⁴·⁵ ≈ 32,000 times that of milk at pH 6.5 —
    every single pH unit is a tenfold jump. <strong>Earthquakes</strong> (Richter/moment
    magnitude): each whole number is about 32 times more energy released — a magnitude 7 is not
    "a bit worse" than a 6, it is a different category of event. <strong>Decibels</strong> (see
    the Waves chapter) and <strong>stellar magnitudes</strong> work the same way. And in data
    work, plotting on log axes is the standard trick for straightening exponential
    relationships: Moore's law — transistor counts doubling every two years — is a straight line
    only on a log plot, which is precisely how such "laws" are discovered.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="drop the marker — the earthquake ratio" widget="place" config={expQuakePlace} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Simplify 8^(2/3), and say which definition forces the answer.</li>
    <li>An investment grows 7% per year. Roughly how long will it take to double, and which rule gave the estimate?</li>
    <li>Why does log(a + b) refuse to split, while log(ab) splits happily?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The cube root of 8 is 2, and 2 squared is 4; a^(m/n) means
    root first, then power, because the definition must keep the index laws true. (2) About
    ten years — the rule of 70: 70 divided by 7. The exact figure is ln 2 / r, roughly 0.693
    divided by 0.07. (3) Multiplying powers adds exponents — b^p times b^q is b^(p+q) — so
    products become sums. Addition has no such structure, so log of a sum has no law; combine
    the addition first, then take the log.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The log is the exponent: log_b(x) = y means b^y = x, and the two curves are mirror images across y = x.</li>
    <li>Zero, negative and fractional exponents are defined to preserve the index laws — not invented as tricks.</li>
    <li>Standard form plus orders of magnitude is how science writes, compares and sanity-checks extreme numbers.</li>
    <li>Constant percentage change is exponential; doubling time is about 70 divided by the percentage rate.</li>
    <li>A half-life halves whatever remains — the asymptote means "never quite zero", not "all gone after two halves".</li>
    <li>Log laws turn products into sums, quotients into differences, powers into coefficients; log of a sum has no law.</li>
    <li>pH, earthquake magnitude and decibels are logarithmic scales: one step means a fixed ratio — roughly tenfold for pH, about thirty-two-fold in energy per magnitude unit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The natural continuation is the intermediate Derivative lesson, whose section on e^x
    explains why that base dominates — and the Sequences and Series lesson next door shows the
    discrete cousin of the same compound-growth story. Trigonometry completes the intermediate
    families of functions.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Reading the graphs like a professional.</strong> Every exponential graph
    A = A₀·bˣ + c is the widget's curves with knobs: b controls growth (b above 1) or decay
    (b between 0 and 1), the multiplier A₀ scales it, and adding a constant c slides the whole
    curve vertically — which moves the asymptote too (a cooling cup of tea approaches room
    temperature, not zero: Newton's law of cooling is A₀·bᵗ plus room temperature). The two
    features to check on any exponential model are the <strong>y-intercept</strong> (the
    starting amount, at x = 0) and the <strong>asymptote</strong> (the level it approaches but
    never reaches). If a "growth curve" in the news flattens toward a nonzero line, that line
    is the model telling you its carrying capacity — logistics, not exponential, and a different
    equation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why e is natural.</strong> Compound £1 at 100% annual interest, credited n times a
    year: (1 + 1/n)ⁿ. As n grows — 2, 10, 1000, 1,000,000 — the payout climbs toward a limit:
    e ≈ 2.71828. The sequence 2.00, 2.59, 2.717, 2.71828… is the number's birth certificate.
    e is the base where the exponential's growth rate <em>equals</em> its value: d/dx eˣ = eˣ,
    the unique self-replicating function (the derivative chapter's surprise). That is why
    calculus, physics and statistics write their exponentials in e and their inverses as ln —
    natural log is the one whose calculus comes out clean (d/dx ln x = 1/x).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Log laws are exponent laws, proved in three lines.</strong> Let p = log_b a and q =
    log_b c, so bᵖ = a and b^q = c. Multiply: ac = bᵖ · b^q = bᵖ⁺^q. Reading the exponent back:
    log_b(ac) = p + q. One proof generates all three laws — a good exercise in seeing that the
    "rules of logs" were never new rules at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Logs, information and digits.</strong> log₂ measures information in bits (a yes/no
    answer is 1 bit; distinguishing 1,024 equally likely options takes log₂1,024 = 10 bits) —
    information theory, file compression and the "sorting takes n·log n comparisons" result in
    computer science all live on this scale. Benford's law adds a curious empirical coda: in
    many real datasets (river lengths, tax returns, street addresses), about 30% of numbers
    begin with digit 1 and under 5% with digit 9 — a consequence of data spanning orders of
    magnitude, and in the real world, a forensic tool for detecting fabricated accounts.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The log as a bridge.</strong> Before calculators, log tables (published 1614, by
    Napier) reduced multiplication to addition and made a century of astronomy computable by
    hand — Kepler's planetary tables were built on them. After calculators, logs lost the
    arithmetic job and kept the conceptual one: any time measurement spans scales (pH, dB,
    stellar brightness, network latency percentiles, pandemic growth curves), the honest
    picture is logarithmic. Learning to <em>think</em> in logs — "how many factors of ten?" —
    is the durable skill this chapter exists to install.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Algebra and Trigonometry (ch. 6, Exponential and Logarithmic Functions); Maor, e: The Story of a Number; USGS earthquake magnitude FAQ; IUPAC pH convention. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
